#lang palimpsest
#mode rewrite-then-run
#fuel 2000000
#caps { rewrite: [self] }

// ctmu-containment.pal — the CTMU's dual containment relation (topological
// vs. descriptive), as a battery of checked theorems over Palimpsest's own
// term algebra. Definitions and full commentary live in ../lib/ctmu.pal;
// this file states and CHECKS three theorems (T1, T2, T3) about them, then
// rewrites its own `main` line down to the computed report and becomes a
// quine — the descriptive layer (the rules below) generates exactly the
// term that ends up as the file's own topological body.
//
// Run once: every check below gets computed for real (status: WROTE).
// Run again: the file is already its own fixed point (status: FIXED POINT).

import "../lib/ctmu.pal"

strategy solve = outermost(prim + rules)

// T1 — topological containment is BOUNDED: a proper part is always
// strictly smaller than its container. "Cupboard contains clothes."
//   topcontains?(cupboard, shirt)          -> true,  a proper part
//   topcontains?(cupboard, cupboard)       -> false, nothing contains itself
//   topcontains?(cupboard, hat)            -> false, hat isn't there at all
//   t1-holds?(cupboard, shirt)             -> true, and CHECKED, not asserted
//
// T2 — descriptive containment is NOT bounded by size: the single fixed,
// finite schema `(family ?xs...)` contains instances of every size. "Clothes
// define what a cupboard IS" — a compact description with unbounded reach,
// which T1's relation structurally cannot have.
//
// T3 — dual containment, the CTMU's actual resolution of the set-of-all-sets
// paradox: ONE object U on BOTH sides of the two DIFFERENT relations at
// once, with no contradiction, because antisymmetry is a property of a
// relation, and these are two different relations. U below literally
// contains a schema shaped exactly like this file's own `subterm-go-eq` rule
// (same arity, same variable structure — see ../lib/ctmu.pal) alongside a
// sample expression that schema (read as a pattern) contains as an
// instance. `dual-contains?` independently re-derives BOTH directions (via
// `subterm?`, structural recursion, and `matches?`, the reflective
// primitive) rather than being told the answer. The mirrored schema is
// named `kin`, not `subterm?`, on purpose — see T3-HAZARD.
//
// T3-HAZARD — an honest negative result, not swept under the rug. If the
// mirrored schema had instead been written with the SAME head symbol as a
// rule that is actually loaded and running in this program (`subterm?`), it
// would NOT stay inert as data: the live rule set treats every subterm of
// `main` as something to normalize, with no notion of "this part is just
// being mentioned, not used". `(subterm? ?s ?t)`, dropped in verbatim below,
// is quietly caught: `subterm?`'s own entry rule matches unconditionally
// (position 3 is a strict `!t`, but forcing an atom is a no-op) and hands
// off to `subterm-go?`, whose `subterm-go-eq` guard fails (the literal
// symbols `?s` and `?t` are not equal to each other) and whose
// `subterm-go-list` case needs the third position to already be a list, so
// it falls through to `subterm-go-atom` and collapses to `false` — silently
// overwritten before it could ever be read back out as "the pattern
// (subterm? ?s ?t)". This is the sharpest evidence in this file for why
// CTMU needs two DIFFERENT containment relations rather than one: the
// instant "currently-quoted description" and "currently-running process"
// share a name, the distinction between mentioning and using collapses —
// exactly the kind of conflation Russell's paradox exploits. Keeping the
// vocabulary of quoted schemas disjoint from the vocabulary of loaded rules
// (as T3 does, and as `verbatim` — see ../src/matcher.rs — does for rules
// constructing fresh data on their own right-hand side) is the actual
// engineering discipline
// the CTMU's two-relation split corresponds to.

main = (ctmu-report (t1 true false false 1 4 true) (t2 true true true true (some (dict (entry xs (list e e e))))) (t3 true) (t3-hazard (mirrored-as-data false) (intended "the pattern (subterm? ?s ?t), preserved as inert data") (actual "silently reduced to false by the live subterm? rules")))

rewrite self with solve
display main with solve
